  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "The position $x$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by $x(t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{{t}^{2}+1}.$ Calculate the velocity at time $t.$",
  "worked_solution": "The velocity is the derivative of the position function $x(t)$ with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{{t}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2t({t}^{2}+1)-{t}^{2}(2t)}{({t}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{t}^{3}+2t-2{t}^{3}}{({t}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{({t}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
